WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.824
The KMP prefix function pi[i] stores the length of the longest proper prefix of S[0..i] that is also a suffix of S[0..i].

00:00:08.824 --> 00:00:14.220
These matching prefix-suffix overlaps are called borders of the substring.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:21.440
Knowing every border lets a pattern matcher slide forward without ever backing up the text pointer.

00:00:21.708 --> 00:00:31.668
To compute pi[i], try extending the previous border of length j equals pi[i minus one] by comparing S[i] with S[j].

00:00:31.668 --> 00:00:40.849
On a mismatch, fall back to the next widest border pi[j minus one] until a match occurs or j reaches zero.

00:00:40.849 --> 00:00:51.588
Because j increases by at most one per step and only drops on fallbacks, the entire table builds in deterministic O(N) time.

00:00:51.875 --> 00:01:01.096
Concatenate the pattern P, a sentinel character absent from both strings, and the text T, then compute the prefix function.

00:01:01.096 --> 00:01:08.593
Every position where pi equals the length of P marks the exact end of a full occurrence in the text.

00:01:08.593 --> 00:01:15.115
Unlike rolling hashes, KMP has zero collision risk and needs no big-integer arithmetic.

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:22.669
The value N minus pi[N minus one] reveals the shortest possible period length k of a string of length N.

00:01:22.669 --> 00:01:28.770
If k divides N evenly, the entire string is formed by repeating its first k characters.

00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:37.186
Counting occurrences of each prefix also follows by accumulating counts from i down to pi[i minus one] in reverse order.
